\title{Sign Misattribution in Step-Independent Agent RL:\\Dual Mechanism Diagnosis and Episode-Level Fix}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Multi-turn agent reinforcement learning requires credit assignment across long interaction trajectories, yet current frameworks default to step-independent windowing that decomposes trajectories into independently optimized windows. We show this introduces a fundamental credit assignment pathology driven by two mechanisms: \textit{policy information loss}, which truncates context and imposes a constant performance ceiling, and \textit{advantage sign misattribution}, which causes gradient updates to reflect episode outcomes rather than local action quality, producing cumulative degradation. At a baseline success rate of $p{=}12.5\%$ with group size $K{=}8$, approximately 80.9\% of gradient updates carry incorrect sign---confirmed empirically by a 52\% credit agreement rate indistinguishable from random chance. A $2{\times}2$ factorial experiment separates the mechanisms: eliminating sign misattribution converts catastrophic collapse into bounded learning; further removing information loss enables generalization. Episode-level GRPO, which treats the full trajectory as a single optimization sample, simultaneously resolves both mechanisms, achieving 23.9\% OOD success at 1.7B where step-independent training collapses to 0\%, and 90.3\% OOD at 8B. Cross-environment validation on ScienceWorld confirms the collapse generalizes beyond ALFWorld.
\end{abstract}

